The error log indicates an assertion failure in `tests/lib/notify/src/main.c` at line 120 within the `test_signal` function: `Assertion failed ...: (rc not equal to -EINVAL)`. This means that `sys_notify_validate(&notify)` returned `0` instead of the expected `-EINVAL`.

Let's examine the `sys_notify_validate` function in `lib/utils/notify.c`:

```c
int sys_notify_validate(struct sys_notify *notify)
{
	int rv = 0;

	if (notify == NULL) {
		return -EINVAL;
	}

	/* Validate configuration based on mode */
	switch (sys_notify_get_method(notify)) {
	case SYS_NOTIFY_METHOD_SPINWAIT:
		break;
	case SYS_NOTIFY_METHOD_CALLBACK:
		if (notify->method.callback == NULL) {
			rv = -EINVAL;
		}
		break;
#ifdef CONFIG_POLL
	case SYS_NOTIFY_METHOD_SIGNAL:
		if (0) { // <-- This is the bug!
			rv = -EINVAL;
		}
		break;
#endif /* CONFIG_POLL */
	default:
		rv = -EINVAL;
		break;
	}

	/* Clear the result here instead of in all callers. */
	if (rv == 0) {
		notify->result = 0;
	}

	return rv;
}
```

The `if (0)` condition within the `SYS_NOTIFY_METHOD_SIGNAL` case is a clear bug. It means that even if `notify->method.signal` were `NULL`, the validation would incorrectly pass (i.e., `rv` would remain `0`). The correct check should be `if (notify->method.signal == NULL)`.

While this specific bug might not directly explain why `sys_notify_validate` returns `0` when `notify` is `memset` with `0xac` (as `0xacacacac & 0x03U` is `0`, which should hit the `default` case and return `-EINVAL`), it is a critical logical error in the validation logic for signal-based notifications. The test `test_signal` explicitly attempts to trigger this condition later by setting `notify.method.signal = NULL;` and then calling `sys_notify_validate`, expecting `-EINVAL`.

Fixing this explicit bug is the most direct and verifiable step. It ensures that `sys_notify_validate` correctly checks for a `NULL` signal pointer when the notification method is `SYS_NOTIFY_METHOD_SIGNAL`.

